WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.470
Think of the folders on your computer.

2
00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:08.210
A folder holds files and other folders

3
00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:10.750
which hold more folders, and so on

4
00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:12.220
as deep as you like.

5
00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:14.160
Or a family tree.

6
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.080
Or a company's org chart.

7
00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.340
Or the structure of a web page.

8
00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:19.930
All of them are trees.

9
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:22.069
So here is a question.

10
00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:24.001
How do you visit every item

11
00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:26.102
in a tree exactly once?

12
00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.003
And does it matter in which

13
00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:29.240
order you visit them?

14
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.970
This chapter is about trees

15
00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:33.860
and how to search them.

16
00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:35.847
In this first video we'll learn

17
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:37.600
the vocabulary of trees

18
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.320
four ways to walk through one

19
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.810
and why the order of the walk matters.

20
00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:45.300
Here is a tree with seven nodes.

21
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.820
The node at the top is the root.

22
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.780
Every other node has exactly one parent

23
00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:52.190
the node directly above it.

24
00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.610
A node can have children.

25
00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:57.630
A node with no children is a leaf.

26
00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:00.970
Here the leaves are twenty, forty

27
00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:02.540
sixty and eighty.

28
00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:05.268
The depth of a node is the number

29
00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:06.820
of steps down from the root.

30
00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:08.380
The root has depth zero

31
00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.600
its children have depth one

32
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.450
and these four leaves have depth two.

33
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.770
The height of the tree is its largest depth

34
00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:18.650
which here is two.

35
00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:21.776
A tree with a single node has height zero

36
00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:23.943
and by convention an empty tree

37
00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:25.680
has height minus one.

38
00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:27.620
In a binary tree

39
00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.920
every node has at most two children

40
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.440
called left and right.

41
00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:32.520
And any node

42
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.120
together with everything below it

43
00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.350
is a subtree.

44
00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:38.620
This gives us a recursive definition.

45
00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:41.313
A binary tree is either empty

46
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.455
or it is a root together with a left

47
00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:46.440
subtree and a right subtree

48
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.570
and both of those are binary trees again.

49
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.640
That should remind you of Chapter four

50
00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.113
a big problem handed to smaller

51
00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:56.380
copies of itself.

52
00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:57.931
Almost every algorithm on

53
00:01:57.990 --> 00:01:59.170
trees has this shape

54
00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:00.831
do something with the root

55
00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:02.722
and let recursion take care

56
00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:04.040
of the two subtrees.

57
00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:05.910
Now the walk.

58
00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:08.179
There are three classic orders

59
00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.962
and the only difference between them

60
00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:12.100
is when you visit the root.

61
00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:15.480
In preorder, you visit the root first

62
00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:16.880
then the left subtree

63
00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.120
then the right subtree.

64
00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.560
Here that gives fifty, thirty, twenty

65
00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:25.530
forty, seventy, sixty, eighty.

66
00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:27.210
In inorder

67
00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.370
you visit the left subtree first

68
00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:31.850
then the root, then the right subtree

69
00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:36.810
twenty, thirty, forty, fifty, sixty

70
00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.550
seventy, eighty.

71
00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.770
Look at that: the keys come out sorted.

72
00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:43.590
That is no accident

73
00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:46.280
and in the next video we will see why.

74
00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:49.824
In postorder, both subtrees come first and

75
00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:54.300
the root comes last: twenty, forty, thirty

76
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.910
sixty, eighty, seventy, fifty.

77
00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.130
There is a fourth order that ignores

78
00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:02.570
the recursive structure.

79
00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:05.150
Level order visits the root

80
00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:07.130
then everything at depth one

81
00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:10.370
then everything at depth two, and so on

82
00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:15.850
fifty, thirty, seventy, twenty, forty

83
00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:17.380
sixty, eighty.

84
00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:20.070
Does the order really matter?

85
00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.510
Take an arithmetic expression

86
00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:25.710
three plus four, times, five minus two.

87
00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:28.670
As a tree, every operator is a node

88
00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:31.160
and the numbers are leaves.

89
00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:33.487
Walk this tree in postorder

90
00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.680
and you get three, four, plus, five, two

91
00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.080
minus, times.

92
00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:40.856
Every operator comes right

93
00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.540
after its two operands

94
00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.180
so no parentheses are needed.

95
00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:46.280
A stack can evaluate it.

96
00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:47.800
Push three and four

97
00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.660
and plus turns them into seven.

98
00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:51.440
Push five and two

99
00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:53.640
and minus turns them into three.

100
00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.550
Times gives twenty-one.

101
00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.110
Now walk the same tree in inorder.

102
00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:04.930
You get three, plus, four, times, five

103
00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:06.630
minus, two.

104
00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:08.530
The parentheses are gone

105
00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:11.350
and read normally this means something else

106
00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:14.710
three plus twenty, minus two.

107
00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:17.990
Preorder gives yet another sequence: times

108
00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:23.780
plus, three, four, minus, five, two.

109
00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.700
Same tree, same nodes

110
00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:27.860
three different results.

111
00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:31.250
The order of the walk is the whole point.

112
00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.560
How does a computer perform these walks?

113
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.414
The recursive versions use the call stack

114
00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:39.815
every recursive call is a frame that

115
00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:41.830
remembers where to continue.

116
00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.224
We can take the call stack away

117
00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:46.410
and use a stack of our own.

118
00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:48.650
For preorder: push the root.

119
00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:52.030
Then repeat: pop a node, visit it

120
00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:54.042
and push its right child and

121
00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:55.290
then its left child

122
00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:57.540
so that the left child is popped first.

123
00:04:57.890 --> 00:05:00.540
Level order uses a queue instead.

124
00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:01.660
Visit a node

125
00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:03.428
and add its children at the back.

126
00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:04.824
The node that has waited

127
00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.120
longest is always next.

128
00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:09.940
A stack, last in first out, goes deep.

129
00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:13.130
A queue, first in first out, goes wide.

130
00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:14.690
Either way

131
00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:17.370
every node is visited exactly once.

132
00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:19.550
For a tree with n nodes

133
00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:22.280
a walk takes time proportional to n.

134
00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:23.990
To sum up.

135
00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:27.470
A tree has a root, parents and children

136
00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:30.166
leaves, depth and height.

137
00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:32.916
A binary tree is a root with

138
00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:35.020
two smaller binary trees.

139
00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:38.840
There are three depth-first walks, preorder

140
00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:40.520
inorder and postorder

141
00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:43.680
and one breadth-first walk, level order.

142
00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:46.520
Recursion or a stack gives the first three

143
00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:48.570
a queue gives the last.

144
00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:51.150
In the next video we make the inorder

145
00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:53.410
walk come out sorted on purpose

146
00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:55.480
with binary search trees.

147
00:05:56.107 --> 00:05:57.155
Now let's build this tree

148
00:05:57.197 --> 00:05:58.787
in Java and walk it.

149
00:05:59.367 --> 00:06:02.277
A node is a small generic class.

150
00:06:02.277 --> 00:06:03.937
The key has type K

151
00:06:03.937 --> 00:06:07.157
so the same class holds integers, strings

152
00:06:07.157 --> 00:06:08.297
or anything else.

153
00:06:08.297 --> 00:06:11.847
The key, the left child and the right child.

154
00:06:12.197 --> 00:06:14.466
null means an empty subtree

155
00:06:14.547 --> 00:06:16.412
so a leaf is a node whose

156
00:06:16.487 --> 00:06:18.157
two children are null.

157
00:06:18.737 --> 00:06:20.367
Here is preorder.

158
00:06:20.367 --> 00:06:22.807
If the node is null, return.

159
00:06:22.807 --> 00:06:25.067
Otherwise visit the key

160
00:06:25.067 --> 00:06:26.847
walk the left subtree

161
00:06:26.847 --> 00:06:28.337
and walk the right subtree.

162
00:06:28.687 --> 00:06:30.802
Inorder and postorder move

163
00:06:30.877 --> 00:06:32.687
only the visit line.

164
00:06:33.037 --> 00:06:35.227
The visit action is a Consumer

165
00:06:35.227 --> 00:06:37.747
so the same method can print keys

166
00:06:37.747 --> 00:06:39.677
collect them into a list, or count them.

167
00:06:40.257 --> 00:06:43.687
Running it on our tree: size seven

168
00:06:43.687 --> 00:06:49.707
height two, leaves twenty, forty, sixty

169
00:06:49.707 --> 00:06:50.857
eighty.

170
00:06:51.207 --> 00:06:53.479
The four walks print the same sequences

171
00:06:53.537 --> 00:06:54.837
as in the last part

172
00:06:54.837 --> 00:06:57.087
in the square brackets of a Java list.

173
00:06:57.667 --> 00:06:59.617
Height is recursive too.

174
00:06:59.617 --> 00:07:02.086
An empty tree has height minus one

175
00:07:02.157 --> 00:07:04.305
and any other tree is one more

176
00:07:04.377 --> 00:07:05.957
than its taller child.

177
00:07:05.957 --> 00:07:07.947
A leaf comes out as zero.

178
00:07:08.527 --> 00:07:10.157
With our own stack

179
00:07:10.157 --> 00:07:12.437
the code uses an array deque.

180
00:07:12.437 --> 00:07:15.137
Java also has a class called Stack

181
00:07:15.137 --> 00:07:16.577
but it is a legacy class

182
00:07:16.577 --> 00:07:19.367
so the array deque is the recommended stack.

183
00:07:19.717 --> 00:07:21.987
Push every node while going left.

184
00:07:21.987 --> 00:07:23.907
Pop one, visit it

185
00:07:23.907 --> 00:07:25.727
then move to its right child.

186
00:07:25.727 --> 00:07:28.077
Push and pop work at the front of the deque.

187
00:07:28.657 --> 00:07:30.359
Level order uses the same

188
00:07:30.427 --> 00:07:32.227
array deque as a queue.

189
00:07:32.227 --> 00:07:33.927
We add the children at the back

190
00:07:33.927 --> 00:07:36.537
and poll takes the next node from the front.

191
00:07:37.117 --> 00:07:38.643
For the expression tree

192
00:07:38.707 --> 00:07:40.584
the keys are a mix of integers

193
00:07:40.647 --> 00:07:42.098
and operator strings

194
00:07:42.167 --> 00:07:44.982
so the tree holds Object keys and

195
00:07:45.067 --> 00:07:46.627
we cast when we read them.

196
00:07:46.687 --> 00:07:48.153
In real code you would give

197
00:07:48.207 --> 00:07:49.307
the nodes a proper type

198
00:07:49.307 --> 00:07:50.557
but the idea is the same.

199
00:07:50.907 --> 00:07:52.877
Evaluate is postorder.

200
00:07:52.877 --> 00:07:54.503
It prints twenty-one.

201
00:07:54.577 --> 00:07:57.180
The inorder walk prints three plus

202
00:07:57.257 --> 00:07:59.957
four times five minus two

203
00:07:59.957 --> 00:08:03.827
without parentheses; toInfix puts them back.

204
00:08:04.407 --> 00:08:06.597
Three common mistakes.

205
00:08:06.597 --> 00:08:09.317
First, forgetting the null check

206
00:08:09.397 --> 00:08:12.443
the call node dot key on a null reference

207
00:08:12.517 --> 00:08:14.607
throws a null pointer exception.

208
00:08:14.957 --> 00:08:17.510
Second, mixing up height in edges

209
00:08:17.587 --> 00:08:20.027
and height in nodes.

210
00:08:20.027 --> 00:08:21.527
Our code counts edges

211
00:08:21.527 --> 00:08:23.997
so a single node has height zero.

212
00:08:24.347 --> 00:08:26.729
Third, deep recursion.

213
00:08:26.837 --> 00:08:28.678
The recursive height of a chain

214
00:08:28.737 --> 00:08:30.591
of five hundred nodes works.

215
00:08:30.657 --> 00:08:33.171
On a chain of a million nodes the same

216
00:08:33.237 --> 00:08:35.297
code throws a StackOverflowError

217
00:08:35.297 --> 00:08:37.707
and the iterative version still works.

218
00:08:38.287 --> 00:08:40.657
Java's library does contain trees

219
00:08:40.657 --> 00:08:42.842
but you never see the nodes.

220
00:08:42.917 --> 00:08:44.393
TreeMap and TreeSet are

221
00:08:44.457 --> 00:08:46.017
balanced search trees

222
00:08:46.017 --> 00:08:47.798
specifically red-black trees

223
00:08:47.857 --> 00:08:49.112
and we will build up to them

224
00:08:49.157 --> 00:08:50.487
in the next chapter.

225
00:08:50.837 --> 00:08:53.051
HashMap and PriorityQueue are

226
00:08:53.127 --> 00:08:54.147
different structures

227
00:08:54.147 --> 00:08:56.227
for Chapters seven and eight.

228
00:08:56.227 --> 00:08:58.939
There is no general-purpose tree class

229
00:08:59.007 --> 00:09:00.454
because every tree you need

230
00:09:00.507 --> 00:09:02.117
has its own rules.

231
00:09:02.697 --> 00:09:06.767
In Java: a generic node, null for empty

232
00:09:06.767 --> 00:09:08.587
recursion for the walks

233
00:09:08.587 --> 00:09:12.017
and an array deque as stack or queue.

234
00:09:12.367 --> 00:09:15.247
Next: binary search trees.

